1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:02,430
For this video,
we're going to start

2
00:00:02,430 --> 00:00:04,275
talking about
process management,

3
00:00:04,275 --> 00:00:06,300
how Linux manages processes.

4
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:09,825
This will be Linux system
programming chapter 5 content.

5
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:12,090
Learning objectives
for this video,

6
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:13,980
we're going to talk
about what a process is.

7
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:15,570
We've already talked
about that a little bit

8
00:00:15,570 --> 00:00:17,220
in the context of
processes and threads;

9
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:18,765
we're going to get into
a little more detail

10
00:00:18,765 --> 00:00:20,340
about what a process is.

11
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:22,260
We're going to talk
about PATH and how

12
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:24,255
that relates to
process execution,

13
00:00:24,255 --> 00:00:26,060
and then we're going to
talk about the system

14
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,115
calls you can use to
invoke a process,

15
00:00:28,115 --> 00:00:29,885
so that includes execl,

16
00:00:29,885 --> 00:00:32,320
fork, wait, and system.

17
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,295
What is a process?

18
00:00:34,295 --> 00:00:36,950
A process is really just a
running program in memory,

19
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:40,835
so it's a binary image of
the program that you built.

20
00:00:40,835 --> 00:00:42,650
It also has that

21
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:44,480
virtualized memory instance
that we talked about

22
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:45,500
before when we talked about

23
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:47,885
the differences between
process and threads.

24
00:00:47,885 --> 00:00:50,120
It's a collection of files,

25
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:52,775
files that are being
accessed by the process,

26
00:00:52,775 --> 00:00:54,710
that are based on

27
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:56,360
a unique file handle

28
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,585
that is going to be
managed by the kernel,

29
00:00:58,585 --> 00:01:00,860
like we talked about in
our previous videos.

30
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:03,500
There'll be an associated
user with the process,

31
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:04,775
the person who started it,

32
00:01:04,775 --> 00:01:06,860
or the person under

33
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,260
whose credentials the process
is currently running,

34
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:12,450
and then one or more
threads of execution.

35
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:15,480
The process is uniquely

36
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:17,490
identified by the
process identifier,

37
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:19,400
and that process identifier is

38
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,710
valid at a single point in time.

39
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:23,840
There's always a map
in the kernel between

40
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,040
a process identifier and

41
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:27,085
a given process at
a moment in time.

42
00:01:27,085 --> 00:01:29,115
However, that process ID

43
00:01:29,115 --> 00:01:32,490
is reallocated and
reused by the kernel.

44
00:01:32,490 --> 00:01:34,940
If you start a process
and then you stop it,

45
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:36,515
and then you start a
different process,

46
00:01:36,515 --> 00:01:38,540
there's a chance that
process ID is going to get

47
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:41,065
reallocated later to
a different process.

48
00:01:41,065 --> 00:01:43,520
There are a few special
process IDs that

49
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,965
are always used for
specific purposes.

50
00:01:45,965 --> 00:01:47,780
One is the idle process,

51
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:49,550
which is process ID 0.

52
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:51,410
There's one of
these per processor

53
00:01:51,410 --> 00:01:53,510
and it handles things
like power control,

54
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:56,590
things that are common
to the entire system.

55
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:00,080
There's also the init process
which is process ID 1,

56
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:01,430
and that's the one
that kicks off

57
00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:04,100
all the services on

58
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,385
startup and does the basics
system initialization.

59
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:09,740
It would also launch
a login program that

60
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:12,930
you could interact with
to log into the system.

61
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:20,050
If we want to execute
a different program

62
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:22,545
from inside our
user space program,

63
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:24,730
the way we do that
through POSIX API,

64
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:26,005
there's a couple
of different ways.

65
00:02:26,005 --> 00:02:27,760
But one of them is execl,

66
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:31,130
and the execl family
of functions.

67
00:02:31,130 --> 00:02:33,460
The important thing
to know about execl

68
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:35,335
is that it's going to

69
00:02:35,335 --> 00:02:39,175
replace the current running
process with a new context,

70
00:02:39,175 --> 00:02:41,650
and that new context will
be based on whatever

71
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:44,170
you're calling as an
argument to execl.

72
00:02:44,170 --> 00:02:47,690
In this case, since
we're calling bin vi,

73
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:51,190
whatever process is
calling this, the memory,

74
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:54,475
the content is going to
get completely swapped out

75
00:02:54,475 --> 00:02:59,475
and the bin vi application
is going to run instead.

76
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:01,650
So execl for this reason,

77
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:03,710
it would not return on success.

78
00:03:03,710 --> 00:03:06,260
If it's successful, it
will have completely

79
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:09,310
replaced the process and
run whatever you specified.

80
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:10,835
It's going to change pretty much

81
00:03:10,835 --> 00:03:14,090
everything in the
current process image,

82
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:15,710
with the exception of the pid,

83
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:17,360
priority, owning
user and groups.

84
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:19,175
All that's going
to stay the same,

85
00:03:19,175 --> 00:03:20,480
but then it's just
going to swap out

86
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,285
the content with the new
process you specified.

87
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,805
There's a few different variants

88
00:03:25,805 --> 00:03:28,070
of execl that you can call,

89
00:03:28,070 --> 00:03:30,125
execlp, execle,

90
00:03:30,125 --> 00:03:32,930
and you can read more
about them in chapter 5,

91
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:37,970
but they differ about how
arguments are specified to

92
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:40,550
the function and also whether

93
00:03:40,550 --> 00:03:41,900
environment is specified and

94
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,110
used and whether
PATH is included.

95
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:46,610
This means the bash environment,

96
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:48,185
the scripts that are
sent in the environment,

97
00:03:48,185 --> 00:03:50,480
including PATH, whether those

98
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,030
are relevant to the
execution of the program.

99
00:03:53,030 --> 00:03:54,470
We'll get into a
little more detail

100
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:56,395
about that in the coming slides.

101
00:03:56,395 --> 00:03:59,100
First of all, what is
the PATH variable?

102
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:00,920
If you've done a little
bit of work with Linux,

103
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:02,360
you might already be used to

104
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,640
the PATH variable or at
least have heard of it.

105
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:07,250
But really the PATH
variable is just telling

106
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:09,140
the shell all the
different places

107
00:04:09,140 --> 00:04:11,170
that it should look
for executables.

108
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:13,040
Here's an example
of what I see in

109
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,700
my system when I echo
out the PATH variable.

110
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:20,460
You can see it's just a colon
separated list of paths.

111
00:04:20,870 --> 00:04:23,810
We've taken it for granted
in the past that if

112
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:26,110
I type a command like ls,

113
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:29,025
it works without needing
to specify a full PATH.

114
00:04:29,025 --> 00:04:30,860
Well, the way that
work happen is

115
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:32,815
because the PATH that ls

116
00:04:32,815 --> 00:04:34,320
is on happens to be in

117
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:36,880
the PATH argument
used by the system.

118
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:38,330
If I do which ls,

119
00:04:38,330 --> 00:04:40,625
that'll tell me the
ls that I'm using.

120
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:43,480
You can see the full
PATH is user bin ls,

121
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:45,560
and if I look back at
the PATH variable,

122
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:48,590
I can see user bin is
in that path variable.

123
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:50,300
That's how the lookup is working

124
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:51,380
and that's why I don't need to

125
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:55,360
specify ls with a full PATH
in order to execute it.

126
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,460
You need to be careful with PATH

127
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:00,170
because there are ways that it

128
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:02,270
could potentially get you into

129
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:03,290
trouble when you aren't

130
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:05,585
specifying the full
PATH of executables.

131
00:05:05,585 --> 00:05:07,730
The book talks a little
bit about this as well,

132
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:09,920
but PATH injection can happen

133
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:12,620
when someone's able to
modify your PATH and

134
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:15,365
redirect a request for

135
00:05:15,365 --> 00:05:17,480
a application without a PATH to

136
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:20,090
someplace you probably
don't want it redirected.

137
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:21,890
For instance, if some hacker was

138
00:05:21,890 --> 00:05:23,810
able to change my
PATH to put home

139
00:05:23,810 --> 00:05:29,415
hacker ahead of my
user bin directory.

140
00:05:29,415 --> 00:05:31,790
Then I tried to execute ls.

141
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:33,670
I would potentially be executing

142
00:05:33,670 --> 00:05:36,020
home hacker ls instead
of user bin ls,

143
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:38,320
which you could see it
would be a problem.

144
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:41,770
The solution to this
is to avoid relying

145
00:05:41,770 --> 00:05:45,080
on PATH and instead use
absolute path for executables.

146
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:47,495
This is one case where it
actually becomes easier

147
00:05:47,495 --> 00:05:50,230
for us because we're building
in the case of embedded,

148
00:05:50,230 --> 00:05:52,960
rebuilding our root file
system from scratch so we know

149
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:54,550
exactly where stuff's going to

150
00:05:54,550 --> 00:05:56,360
be located in the
row file system.

151
00:05:56,360 --> 00:06:00,770
So we can just invoke it
directly based on absolute path.

152
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:04,995
Another related Posix function

153
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:08,590
that is useful in the
context of processes.

154
00:06:08,590 --> 00:06:10,930
Here is fork, so fork where

155
00:06:10,930 --> 00:06:12,640
exec is going to completely

156
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:14,610
replace the current
process image,

157
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:17,350
fork is going to create
a new process for us.

158
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:19,630
The new process is
going to be running

159
00:06:19,630 --> 00:06:21,795
in the same image as
the current process,

160
00:06:21,795 --> 00:06:23,480
but it's essentially
going to be a child

161
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:25,390
of the current running process.

162
00:06:25,390 --> 00:06:27,970
It'll create a new PID
for that child and

163
00:06:27,970 --> 00:06:30,525
then kickoff the process,

164
00:06:30,525 --> 00:06:33,425
running the content in

165
00:06:33,425 --> 00:06:35,625
the application that's
specific to the child.

166
00:06:35,625 --> 00:06:39,155
This is often the method
that's used to start daemons.

167
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:40,450
The daemon is really just a

168
00:06:40,450 --> 00:06:41,810
process running in
the background.

169
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:43,690
We'll give a specific
example about

170
00:06:43,690 --> 00:06:46,470
how to use fork with daemons.

171
00:06:46,470 --> 00:06:49,585
The other thing to know
about fork is that it uses

172
00:06:49,585 --> 00:06:51,480
copy-on-write for any changes

173
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:53,140
to the process memory space.

174
00:06:53,140 --> 00:06:56,650
When the parent forks
into the child,

175
00:06:56,650 --> 00:06:58,495
there might be local variables,

176
00:06:58,495 --> 00:07:00,740
kernel resources,
open file handles

177
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:03,925
all this stuff that was
created in the parent process.

178
00:07:03,925 --> 00:07:06,925
The child process is going to
have a copy of all of those

179
00:07:06,925 --> 00:07:08,500
because the memory space

180
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:10,575
being essentially
cloned at start.

181
00:07:10,575 --> 00:07:13,660
However, if the child starts
changing those values,

182
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:16,425
then those values are going
to differ from the parent.

183
00:07:16,425 --> 00:07:20,105
But behind the scenes, Linux,

184
00:07:20,105 --> 00:07:22,250
rather than making a
complete separate copy

185
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:26,975
of the entire environment
between these two processes,

186
00:07:26,975 --> 00:07:28,865
it'll use what's
called copy-on-write,

187
00:07:28,865 --> 00:07:32,515
meaning initially they will
share exactly the same copy.

188
00:07:32,515 --> 00:07:35,760
Then if the parent or
the child writes to

189
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:38,165
the local variable or

190
00:07:38,165 --> 00:07:40,350
changes the state of
open file handles.

191
00:07:40,350 --> 00:07:41,690
It's only at that
point that they'll

192
00:07:41,690 --> 00:07:43,095
get their own separate copy.

193
00:07:43,095 --> 00:07:45,085
This is just something that's
going on behind the scenes,

194
00:07:45,085 --> 00:07:47,550
but it's interesting
to know about.

195
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:54,110
Here's a really simple example
of a fork application,

196
00:07:54,110 --> 00:07:55,620
and you can see the
link to this here

197
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:57,500
on the bottom of the slide.

198
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:01,120
We're just going to
do a fork call here,

199
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,095
we can check the return
of the fork to try to

200
00:08:03,095 --> 00:08:05,580
figure out whether we're
the parent or the child.

201
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:07,770
Then we can print out
whether we're the parent or

202
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:11,610
the child process here
in this print statement.

203
00:08:11,610 --> 00:08:14,120
If you run this, you'll see
something like this where you

204
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:17,020
can get the child PID
that was created.

205
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:19,400
You'll see the
hello messages from

206
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:23,190
both the parent and the child
after the process splits.

207
00:08:23,610 --> 00:08:26,780
The book also mentioned
zombie processes.

208
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:31,055
This is a state where when
a child process dies before

209
00:08:31,055 --> 00:08:33,335
parent and before the parent

210
00:08:33,335 --> 00:08:36,325
is informed about the
child terminating.

211
00:08:36,325 --> 00:08:41,690
It's listed as zombie status
in the process status.

212
00:08:41,690 --> 00:08:44,469
When would the parent
not have been notified?

213
00:08:44,469 --> 00:08:46,990
It's really when the
parent hasn't waited or

214
00:08:46,990 --> 00:08:50,945
hasn't obtained the status
about the child process.

215
00:08:50,945 --> 00:08:55,705
One of the ways to do that is
using the weight POSIX API.

216
00:08:55,705 --> 00:08:57,995
So you can use weight
from the parent to

217
00:08:57,995 --> 00:09:01,055
obtain information about
terminated children.

218
00:09:01,055 --> 00:09:04,810
This would not only
obtain information,

219
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:09,410
but also wait for the child
process to terminate.

220
00:09:09,410 --> 00:09:11,260
Based on the response from wait,

221
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:12,930
you can figure out
the reason that

222
00:09:12,930 --> 00:09:15,030
the child process terminated.

223
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:19,779
You can also get the return
code from the process.

224
00:09:19,779 --> 00:09:25,160
There's some additional options
here about, for instance,

225
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:28,000
specifying the process
ID to wait for

226
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,530
a specific process if

227
00:09:29,530 --> 00:09:32,420
the process is starting
multiple children.

228
00:09:32,490 --> 00:09:36,950
We've discussed replacing
a process with exec,

229
00:09:36,950 --> 00:09:38,950
launching a new
child process with

230
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:41,720
fork and waiting for
completion with wait.

231
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:44,315
How would we, if we
wanted to launch

232
00:09:44,315 --> 00:09:47,575
a completely new process and
then wait for completion,

233
00:09:47,575 --> 00:09:50,795
not replacing our current
process, how would we do that?

234
00:09:50,795 --> 00:09:53,270
We just combine
fork, exec and wait.

235
00:09:53,270 --> 00:09:55,470
Fork would create a
new process for us.

236
00:09:55,470 --> 00:09:57,280
Exec inside that new process

237
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:58,535
would replace everything about

238
00:09:58,535 --> 00:10:00,305
the process which is
fine because it's

239
00:10:00,305 --> 00:10:02,720
our child process
rather than a parent.

240
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:06,165
Then wait from the parent

241
00:10:06,165 --> 00:10:07,355
is going to wait
for that child to

242
00:10:07,355 --> 00:10:10,370
complete and get the status.

243
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:13,635
You might say, that
seems complicated.

244
00:10:13,635 --> 00:10:15,650
Is there a less
complicated option?

245
00:10:15,650 --> 00:10:18,200
It turns out there is,
it's called system.

246
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,555
System will do all these for us.

247
00:10:20,555 --> 00:10:21,695
It'll do the fork,

248
00:10:21,695 --> 00:10:23,095
the exec and the wait.

249
00:10:23,095 --> 00:10:24,485
You can just call system

250
00:10:24,485 --> 00:10:27,510
with the command that
you want to run.

251
00:10:27,510 --> 00:10:30,220
It will handle this all for you.

252
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:31,980
But there are a couple
of drawbacks to this,

253
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:33,160
you have to be careful about.

254
00:10:33,160 --> 00:10:35,620
One is that it uses the
PATH so it can have

255
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:39,070
PATH injection concerns like
we talked about previously.

256
00:10:39,070 --> 00:10:40,770
Another thing that can get you

257
00:10:40,770 --> 00:10:42,550
into trouble is that
it runs in the shell,

258
00:10:42,550 --> 00:10:45,060
so it's going to
expand shell input.

259
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:48,490
You want to be careful when
you create this command that

260
00:10:48,490 --> 00:10:50,050
you aren't passing
anything that could

261
00:10:50,050 --> 00:10:52,170
look like a shell variable.

262
00:10:52,170 --> 00:10:55,040
For instance, if you tried
to pass dollar home,

263
00:10:55,040 --> 00:10:56,890
that dollar home
would get expanded by

264
00:10:56,890 --> 00:10:59,020
the shell variable to
your home directory,

265
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:01,020
which might not be
what you wanted.

266
00:11:01,020 --> 00:11:02,615
These are just things
that you need to think

267
00:11:02,615 --> 00:11:03,940
about before you know

268
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:07,570
whether you can use system
in your application.